## Decisions for all 22 candidates

“Main” and “wildcard” are places in this reading selection; they are not a decision about the final three. The order below follows the accepted decision report.

| Essay | Contribution in plain terms | Decision and reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [E074 · When Only Believers Count: Faith and the Evidence of Participation](../essays/selection-20261018/E074.md) | Agreement among participants does not establish that a practice is reliable if those who disagree are excluded from the count in advance. A beginner's departure, a graduate's doubts, and the loss of a skill also answer different questions, so they cannot all be treated alike. | **Wildcard reading place.** If participants who disagree are declared unsuitable solely because they disagree, the agreement of those who remain ceases to provide an independent check on the practice. I include this as one of the two promising wildcard essays: it distinguishes a beginner's departure, a graduate's objection, and the loss of a skill, helping to show whose testimony answers which question. I do not place it among the main eight: the real case examined does not establish selection specifically on the basis of religious agreement, and requirements that combine skill and belief still need to be examined. |
| [F02 · Perfectly Proportioned, Poorly Informed](../essays/selection-20261018/F02.md) | Deleting a source label may leave today's level of confidence unchanged but prevent tomorrow's check from being used properly. A person may then continue to reason without error from the remaining data, even though the quality of the information available to them has declined. | **Main reading place.** Deleting a source label may preserve today's confidence and error-free reasoning from the remaining data, yet make tomorrow's check less useful. I include it among the main eight: the calculation has been checked, and the conclusion is limited to showing that correct use of the retained data alone is insufficient to assess the decision to delete information. All five comparisons with E039 ended in a tie; I choose F02 for its narrower conclusion, which does not require an additional disputed duty to retain information, although the novelty of both essays relative to earlier work remains unresolved. |
| [F05 · Knowing the Experience You Changed](../essays/selection-20261018/F05.md) | An accurate description of a present experience does not yet show what it was like before it was observed. Conclusions about its earlier presence and its earlier absence require different conditions; several accurate descriptions also do not guarantee a complete picture of a single moment. | **Outside this reading selection.** An accurate description of an experience after training in observation does not yet show what the experience was like before the training. I leave it outside the ten for now: the essay correctly distinguishes conclusions about a feature's earlier presence and absence, but does not provide a substantiated account of how training changes the particular experience in dispute. It is also necessary to establish that the actual opposing parties are discussing the same earlier state; without that, the hypothetical example does not yet correct a specific mistaken conclusion. |
| [E039 · Settled, but Still Revisable](../essays/selection-20261018/E039.md) | It is possible to retain a justified conclusion today while deleting information needed for a future check. The essay separates the reasonableness of a later belief from responsibility for the earlier deletion, and defends a narrow condition under which the information must be kept. | **Outside this reading selection.** One can reason correctly today and still delete information needed to check the same question tomorrow. I leave it outside the ten for now, while preserving its tie with F02: the additional duty to retain information depends on a disputed duty to prevent a loss in quality when it can be prevented at no other cost. For the ten, I choose F02's narrower conclusion; preferring E039 would require a persuasive answer to someone who permits inquiry that is good enough without requiring every cost-free opportunity for improvement to be taken. |
| [E098 · Being Able to Stop](../essays/selection-20261018/E098.md) | Another person's warning can help someone stop in time, even if that help was not agreed in advance. But being able to hear, assess, and act on the warning does not make the person the source of someone else's observation or the organizer of the entire protective system. | **Outside this reading selection.** Another person's warning may help someone stop even if no one arranged that help in advance. I leave it outside the ten for now: the essay correctly separates the ability to use a warning from being the source of someone else's observation, but does not fully explain when this is an ability of the person acting rather than an opportunity made available to them. E055 identifies more precisely the condition under which a reviewer's help will work; E098 needs a more convincing distinction between controlling an action and simply regaining control after a prompt. |
| [E012 · When a Remedy Becomes Evidence](../essays/selection-20261018/E012.md) | The fund broke its promise to keep photographs of the damage and then paid compensation for their loss. If its decision is later wrongly treated as evidence that the complaint was false, the fund may have an additional duty to help the claimant defend herself. | **Main reading place.** The fund promised to keep photographs for future disputes, lost them, and paid compensation, but its decision is later mistakenly used as evidence that the complaint was false. I include it among the main eight: the essay identifies a specific duty to help with the claimant's defence that the first payment may not have discharged, and thus goes beyond F01's general requirement to consider complaints fairly. The extent of reasonable help still needs to be examined: the photographs could have shown that the crack was already present, but would not by themselves have proved the contractor was at fault. |
| [E041 · What Foreknowledge Can Take Away](../essays/selection-20261018/E041.md) | A spoiler can remove a distinctive part of encountering a work: the chance to make a reasonable mistake at first and then revise one's own understanding. This can be a loss even when enjoyment and the work's other merits remain. | **Main reading place.** A spoiler may deprive a viewer of the chance to form a reasonable but mistaken initial understanding of a work and then revise that understanding themselves. I include it among the main eight because it identifies a specific possible loss in the experience of a work, whereas E100 does not yet explain why being unable to return to it is valuable in itself. It remains an open question why actually revising one's understanding is more valuable than knowingly imagining such a revision; the essay does not imply a general ban on spoilers. |
| [E050 · What Holding Cannot Tell Us About Letting Go](../essays/selection-20261018/E050.md) | Two systems can behave the same under every way of keeping variables permanently fixed, yet reach opposite outcomes once those constraints are removed. In the model presented, the desired outcome can still be guaranteed by releasing the variables in the right order. | **Main reading place.** In the constructed model, two systems respond identically to every permanent holding of variables fixed, yet may reach opposite outcomes after release. I include it among the main eight: both this example and the proof showing how sequential release can produce the required outcome within the stated class of systems have been checked. Before the final selection, the significance of this limited result and its difference from earlier work still need to be established; the proof does not cover all real systems. |
| [E009 · Before You Tell Us to Try Again](../essays/selection-20261018/E009.md) | If a policy can leave people in a weaker position, its promised protections must still work after that happens. The option to complain offers little help when the person harmed must gather the evidence, pay for the dispute, and secure enforcement of the decision. | **Main reading place.** A policy may weaken a person's position so much that the promised complaint procedure becomes inaccessible to them: they need evidence, money, and enforcement of the decision. I include it among the main eight because the essay traces this entire chain and explains why protections must be assessed under the very conditions in which harm may occur. Before the final selection, it remains to be clarified how reliable such protection must be; the essay does not show that every imperfect procedure rules out the policy. |
| [E079 · What the Verdict Does Not Tell Us](../essays/selection-20261018/E079.md) | The same proportions of choices in an experiment could result from some people excluding a prohibited consideration entirely, or from everyone giving it less weight. Those figures therefore cannot distinguish the two explanations, even though the rule can change decisions in both cases. | **Main reading place.** The same proportions of decisions in an experiment are compatible both with some people entirely excluding a prohibited consideration and with everyone giving it less weight. I include it among the main eight: the calculation provides a specific alternative explanation of the published results and limits the inference that the consideration was entirely excluded from deliberation. This does not refute the claim that people follow rules at all; before the final selection, the statistical conventions of the original study and the novelty of this particular objection still need to be clarified. |
| [E036 · No Penalty for Silence](../essays/selection-20261018/E036.md) | Someone else's diary may reveal that a person was unfairly excluded from a contest. Restoring their place in a lottery where everyone has an equal chance can be permissible, even though the others' chances decrease; giving them an extra advantage for qualities discovered in the diary is a separate decision. | **Outside this reading selection.** A diary may help restore a wrongly excluded person's place in an equal-chance lottery, but that does not yet entitle them to an extra chance of winning because of merits discovered in the diary. I leave it outside the ten for now: the distinction is useful, but the main prohibition on a bonus already follows to a large extent from the independently accepted rule of an equal-chance lottery. In the direct comparison, all five reviews preferred E078 for its more developed explanation of how costs should be allocated; E036 R1 is preserved, and the general case of unequal merit remains unresolved. |
| [F01 · Answerability Without Surveillance](../essays/selection-20261018/F01.md) | If an organization promises compensation for a breach but controls evidence that claimants cannot access, it needs a working process for considering claims. This does not mean it must believe every claim or always pay the maximum amount. | **Outside this reading selection.** An organization promises compensation but itself controls evidence of the breach that the claimant cannot access. I leave it outside the ten for now: the requirement for an accessible process for considering complaints is better justified than the stronger duty to protect a person against the remaining loss. E012 identifies a specific additional duty after compensation has already been paid; F01 needs to clarify when a fair hearing is insufficient and why further help is then required. |
| [F03 · Errors, Repairs, and Evidence about Essence](../essays/selection-20261018/F03.md) | An error in one thought experiment can change our confidence in another, even if the error alone does not tell us whether the second conclusion is true. What matters is how much the two responses depend on a shared way of reasoning; merely pointing to an error cannot settle that. | **Outside this reading selection.** An error discovered in one thought experiment may change confidence in another if both responses depend on a shared way of reasoning. I leave it outside the ten for now: the conditional calculation is correct and includes the subsequent correction of the error, but a real shared cause of the errors in the dispute under discussion has not yet been established, and the proposed experiment has not been conducted. E074 and E078 already distinguish in greater detail between cases that support different conclusions about evidence or costs; F03's slight advantage over F05 does not by itself fill this gap. |
| [F06 · Keeping Refusal Possible](../essays/selection-20261018/F06.md) | If a public system was approved for a trial with a real option to discontinue it, those running it must preserve that option. Without a new decision, they cannot make essential support so dependent on the experiment that continuing it becomes unavoidable. | **Main reading place.** A public system introduced on a trial basis may be made so essential to helping people that the promised option to discontinue it stops working. I include it among the main eight: the essay connects the initial authorization to specific actions—preserving the option to refuse in advance, obtaining new authorization, or remedying the dependence that has been created. This has more practical implications than E047 and S02, but the limits of the duty still need to be clarified for implicit authorization and cases where restoring the option to refuse is too costly. |
| [E078 · Revocable Consent and the Cost of Preserving Choice](../essays/selection-20261018/E078.md) | If one person proposes an optional joint project, the costs arising solely from that proposal cannot automatically be split equally. First, we must establish the fair starting position against which those costs are measured and whether declining is a real option. | **Wildcard reading place.** One participant may change a joint undertaking in a way that forces another to continue helping people, although they could previously have stopped. I include it as one of the two promising wildcard essays: it explains why the person initiating an optional change usually pays to preserve someone else's choice, and why the answer differs for shared costs; all five comparisons preferred it to E036. I do not place it among the main eight because it remains unresolved what initial allocation of duties is fair and when the cost of preserving choice becomes excessive. |
| [F04 · After Agreement Ends: What Denial Can Change](../essays/selection-20261018/F04.md) | When a father denies making a promise and his child stops asking for what was promised, it is not yet clear that the obligation has ended. The child may be tired of arguing or may have accepted a substitute; ending the dispute and ending the right to demand what was promised are different things. | **Outside this reading selection.** A child may stop asking their father for what he promised because they are tired of arguing, not because the obligation has disappeared. I leave it outside the ten for now: the essay convincingly shows that ending a dispute is ambiguous, but its force against the chosen theory depends on an additional step from a change in the relationship to the end of the obligation. The essay acknowledges this step as a separate premise; it needs to show that the opponent is actually committed to accepting it, otherwise the result remains a useful clarification rather than a decisive objection. |
| [E086 · The Inquiry Inside an Invitation to Faith](../essays/selection-20261018/E086.md) | Confidence can help a shared undertaking, but urging people to believe cannot replace the work needed for success or the evidence for a specific promise. The example of checks on banks distinguishes the checks themselves, the correction of problems they uncover, and what can justifiably be promised to the public. | **Outside this reading selection.** An appeal to trust a bank may help it operate, but cannot replace checking the bank's condition and correcting the problems found. I leave it outside the ten for now: the essay usefully separates checking, correcting problems, and making a public promise, but the example examined does not yet change a specific disputed conclusion about what level of confidence may be promised. Reconsideration requires such a case and a justified resolution of it; the general distinction between useful faith and a promise supported by evidence is not enough here. |
| [E100 · The Museum of the Last Second](../essays/selection-20261018/E100.md) | Stopping a work before its last note does not necessarily preserve it: its ending may be part of what it does. This does not imply a ban on recordings or repeat performances, or a general duty to destroy art. | **Outside this reading selection.** Stopping a performance before the last note may lose the work's completion itself rather than preserve the work in its most valuable form. I leave it outside the ten for now: this argument defends completion but does not yet show why the inability to see or perform the work again has artistic value in itself. E041 describes a specific loss in the experience of a work more clearly, and all five comparisons preferred it; E100 does not generally prohibit recordings, repetition, or the preservation of art. |
| [S02 · Who Chooses the Last Citizens’ Assembly?](../essays/selection-20261018/S02.md) | It is possible to convene several citizens' assemblies fairly and still select a convenient answer by repeating deliberation until one gives approval. Fair selection of participants alone does not explain why that particular report should count as the consultation's result. | **Outside this reading selection.** Citizens' assemblies can be convened fairly while a convenient answer is still selected by repeating them until the first approval. I leave it outside the ten for now: the distinction between an assembly's authenticity, the strength of its evidence, and a report's claim to count as the final result is convincing, but the positive rule for choosing the final report is less developed. F06 derives duties from the authorization given more clearly; S02 needs a case where sufficient evidence and permissible political reasons are already present, yet choosing the final report still creates a separate problem. |
| [E037 · Sincere, Forgiven, and Still Unacceptable](../essays/selection-20261018/E037.md) | A woman sincerely apologizes for her mistake but wrongly presents herself as the only person at fault. The person harmed has already forgiven her, yet may still reject that account of blame; an error about another person's responsibility stated separately would not necessarily prevent her from accepting the apology itself. | **Outside this reading selection.** A woman sincerely apologizes but wrongly presents herself as the only person at fault, and the person harmed, who has already forgiven her, refuses to accept that particular account. I leave it outside the ten for now: the example clearly distinguishes forgiveness from accepting an apology, but does not rule out the reply that these are two different acts or an unsuccessful apology. Reconsideration requires an argument for why this reply does not resolve the objection to the theory being examined; insincerity or deliberate lying has not been established here and is not needed for the example. |
| [E055 · Who Gets to Tell the Tool It Is Wrong?](../essays/selection-20261018/E055.md) | A review of an automated decision is unreliable if evidence of an error must pass through the same mechanism that may have wrongly filtered it out. A route for that evidence must remain open; simply having another reviewer guarantees nothing. | **Main reading place.** Evidence of an automated system's error may fail to reach the reviewer if it is filtered by the same mechanism that produced the error. I include it among the main eight: the essay shows why simply adding another reviewer is insufficient and distinguishes help with an individual complaint from a review of the whole system. Unlike E009, this essay specifically examines the route taken by evidence of an error; it remains to justify which errors the review should cover and how reliable it must be. |
| [E047 · Who Gets to Declare the Transformation Complete?](../essays/selection-20261018/E047.md) | A participant may sincerely approve of a program even though their refusal would not have triggered the promised independent review. The option to leave the program does not replace the right to obtain that review while remaining in the program and continuing the discussion. | **Outside this reading selection.** A participant may sincerely approve of a program even though their refusal would not have triggered the promised independent review available without leaving the program. I leave it outside the ten for now: the essay convincingly distinguishes genuine consent from procedural compliance, but after a full new approval its result may amount only to establishing a past violation. F06 shows more clearly what actions are still required; E047 needs to establish when a duty to remedy the situation remains, rather than merely grounds for calling the earlier procedure wrong. |

[Accepted source decisions](../evidence/final-selection/updates/20261018/selection/DECISIONS.json) · [Exact versions and fingerprints](../evidence/final-selection/updates/20261018/manifest.json). The English summaries are editorial translations tied to the corresponding accepted source rows.

## Before this comparison

E036's comparison R1 became its working version before this selection. It gives a clearer account of the promised chances and the difference between correcting an exclusion and granting an additional advantage. Its narrower contribution and the earlier disagreement remain part of the record.

The preparation package then completed its eight targeted tasks and separate checks of the seven later essays. All 22 remained eligible for comparison. E078, E037, and E074 retained explicit limitations; completion did not certify novelty or guarantee a place in the reading selection.

## The agreed balance of reviewers

On October 18, 2026, the user chose a **60/40 balance between neutral reviewers and reviewers using named judge profiles** for the selection from 22 essays to ten. The accepted design assigns three neutral reviews and two profile reviews to each fixed manuscript: **110 individual reviews are planned**. Disputed pairs may receive the same three-to-two allocation, with a limit of ten pairs and 160 reviews overall.

Each review has equal weight within its group. The preliminary summary combines 60% of the mean neutral assessment with 40% of the mean profile assessment. That summary guides closer comparisons; it does not automatically determine the shortlist. Missing reviews remain explicit gaps rather than zero scores or a reason to silently change the balance. The final choice must explain the checked arguments and objections from both groups.

## What a judge-profile agent means here

A profile is a set of reviewing instructions grounded in selected work by a named philosopher. A fresh model session receives the assigned text, common rubric, and that profile. **The project did not fine-tune a separate model for each judge.** The profiles remain experiments in directing attention and criticism; they are not validated predictions of how the real judges will vote.

The accepted allocation uses two distinct profiles per essay from the stored library of 23. Their assignments were fixed before review, with each profile used once or twice across the 44 profile seats. Two profiles give an essay two specified perspectives; they do not represent the full panel. Familiar terminology, agreement with a judge, or citing that judge earns no automatic advantage.

These paragraphs describe the accepted design and its interpretation. The dated completion record separately establishes how much of the planned work was actually accepted.

## What follows

The user reads the packet and the next stage checks the strongest claims, closest prior work, quotations, and remaining objections. Choosing three essays plus two reserves is a later decision. Submission requires a separate explicit instruction.

Completion is bounded to the accepted package. Earlier capture gaps remain in the record; this is not a claim that the complete history of either provider was captured. Exact visible fragments preserve selected text and its source hashes without exporting full native envelopes or hidden reasoning.
